 "result": "This is a Flask-style fork named `tessel`. Paths are under `src/tessel/`.\n\n**How `request`, `session` and `g` get into every template**\n- **Jinja globals:** `create_jinja_environment()` in `app.py:467` (the `rv.globals.update(...)` block at lines 493-503) registers `request`, `session`, `g` and `config` as globals. This is what makes them visible in imported templates.\n- **Default context processor:** `_base_template_vars()` in `templating.py:21` is pre-registered under scope `None` in `template_ctx_injectors`, at `sansio/scaffold.py:182-184`. It swaps in the concrete `g` and, when there is a request, the concrete `request`. `session` stays a proxy so that `session.accessed` gets set.\n- **Run at render time:** `render_template` and `render_template_string` (`templating.py:136` and `:151`) call `_render`, which calls `app.fill_template_scope(ctx, context)` (`app.py:588`). That function runs the processors, app-level first and then the current bundles', and re-applies the values you passed to `render_template` so they win.\n\n**Adding your own variables**\n- **App-wide:** decorate a function with `@app.ctx_injector`. It is defined in `sansio/scaffold.py:550`, which is Flask's `context_processor`. The function returns a dict, and its keys become template variables.\n- **Bundle-scoped:** `@bundle.ctx_injector` applies only to that bundle's views.\n- **Bundle-wide:** `@bundle.app_ctx_injector` (`sansio/bundles.py:645`) applies to every template in the app.\n\nNote that the docstring of `fill_template_scope` says it injects `request`, `session` and `g` itself, but the code only runs the registered processors.",
